# Backwards Compatibility

Any change to this project must remain compatible with the previous released version, or include an explicit transition / compatibility layer so upgrades from older installs do not break.

## Required

- Prefer additive changes (new fields, new endpoints, optional config) over breaking renames or removals.
- When behavior, schema, storage paths, or API contracts must change, add a **compat layer**: migration, dual-read, dual-verify, feature flag, or documented one-time upgrade step that preserves existing data and clients.
- Do not silently enable new defaults (via config deep-merge or similar) that change security or storage semantics unless a stable key/path and migration path already exist.
- Keep external client contracts working across upgrades: web session cookies, session JWTs, opaque API tokens (`Authorization: Bearer`), and query `token` / `access_token` (Comfy Portal, Krita AI Diffusion, headless clients).

## High-risk areas (always plan a transition)

- Authentication and credentials (password hashing/verification, login, MFA, token issuance)
- Database schema, SQLCipher / `encryption_level`, and `SECRET_KEY` / ephemeral key handling
- Config defaults merged into existing `config.json`
- On-disk paths (`MSS_LOGIN_DATA_DIR`, users DB, avatars, workflows)
- HTTP/WebSocket API shapes used by ComfyUI, Comfy Portal, and third-party clients

## Not allowed

- Shipping a change that makes existing passwords, tokens, or encrypted databases unverifiable without a migration or dual-path verifier
- Removing or renaming public routes/fields without a legacy alias for at least one release
- Assuming all installs set `SECRET_KEY` or use a fresh empty database
